# Phase V Specifications - TodoApp Advanced Features

version: "1.0"
phase: "Phase V - Advanced Cloud Deployment"
created: "2026-01-06"
status: "draft"

---

## Part A: Advanced Features

### REQ-001: Recurring Tasks

priority: high
category: "Core Feature"
epic: "Advanced Task Management"

description: |
  Users can create tasks that automatically spawn new instances based on a recurrence pattern.
  This enables habit tracking, recurring chores, and regular reminders without manual creation.

user_story: |
  As a user, I want to create recurring tasks (daily, weekly, monthly) so that I don't have to
  manually create the same task repeatedly.

acceptance_criteria:
  - User can create a task with a recurrence pattern (daily, weekly, monthly, custom)
  - User can specify recurrence end date or number of occurrences
  - System automatically spawns new task instances at the correct time
  - User can complete individual instances without affecting future occurrences
  - User can modify the recurrence pattern (applies to future instances only)
  - User can delete a recurring task (with option to delete all future instances)
  - Completed instances show in task history
  - Recurrence engine runs via Dapr Cron Binding (every 1 minute check)

technical_requirements:
  - New table: recurrence_pattern (id, task_id, frequency, interval, end_date, max_occurrences)
  - Recurrence Service (FastAPI) with Dapr Cron Binding
  - Event publishing: task.spawned event to Kafka
  - MCP tool: add_recurring_task(title, description, pattern, interval, end_date)

test_scenarios:
  - Create daily task, verify spawns at midnight every day
  - Create weekly task (every Monday), verify correct spawning
  - Complete recurring task instance, verify future instances unaffected
  - Delete recurring task, verify future instances cancelled
  - Edit recurrence pattern, verify only future instances affected

---

### REQ-002: Due Dates and Reminders

priority: high
category: "Core Feature"
epic: "Advanced Task Management"

description: |
  Users can set due dates for tasks and receive reminders before the deadline.
  Supports multiple reminder times and notification channels.

user_story: |
  As a user, I want to set due dates for my tasks and get reminded before they're due,
  so I never miss important deadlines.

acceptance_criteria:
  - User can set a due date (date + time) for any task
  - User can configure reminder schedule (e.g., 1 hour before, 1 day before)
  - System sends reminders via in-app notifications (Phase V scope)
  - Overdue tasks are highlighted in the UI
  - User can snooze reminders (reschedule for later)
  - User can mark task complete from reminder notification
  - Reminders respect user timezone

technical_requirements:
  - New columns: todo.due_date (timestamp), todo.reminder_config (jsonb)
  - Reminder Service (FastAPI) with Dapr Cron Binding
  - Event publishing: reminder.triggered event to Kafka
  - Frontend: UI for setting due dates and reminder preferences
  - MCP tool: set_due_date(task_id, due_date, reminder_schedule)

test_scenarios:
  - Set due date for tomorrow 3pm, verify reminder 1 hour before
  - Overdue task shows red highlight in UI
  - Complete task, verify reminder cancelled
  - Snooze reminder, verify it triggers again later
  - Multiple reminders for same task (1 day, 1 hour before)

---

### REQ-003: Task Priorities

priority: medium
category: "Core Feature"
epic: "Advanced Task Management"

description: |
  Users can assign priority levels to tasks (low, medium, high, critical) to organize
  and sort their workload by importance.

user_story: |
  As a user, I want to assign priorities to my tasks so I can focus on the most important
  items first.

acceptance_criteria:
  - User can set priority: low, medium, high, critical
  - Tasks default to medium priority if not specified
  - UI shows priority with visual indicators (colors, icons)
  - User can filter tasks by priority
  - User can sort tasks by priority
  - Priority changes emit events to Kafka

technical_requirements:
  - New column: todo.priority (enum: low, medium, high, critical)
  - Database migration to add priority column with default 'medium'
  - Update Task Service to handle priority in CRUD operations
  - Event publishing: task.priority_changed event to Kafka
  - Frontend: Priority selector component
  - MCP tool: set_priority(task_id, priority)

test_scenarios:
  - Create task with high priority, verify stored correctly
  - Filter tasks to show only critical priority
  - Sort tasks by priority (critical → high → medium → low)
  - Change priority from low to high, verify event published

---

### REQ-004: Task Tags

priority: medium
category: "Core Feature"
epic: "Advanced Task Management"

description: |
  Users can add multiple tags to tasks for flexible categorization and organization.
  Tags enable custom workflows and filtering beyond simple lists.

user_story: |
  As a user, I want to tag my tasks with custom labels (e.g., #work, #personal, #urgent)
  so I can organize and find tasks more easily.

acceptance_criteria:
  - User can add multiple tags to a task
  - Tags are free-form text (user can create new tags on the fly)
  - User can remove tags from a task
  - User can filter tasks by one or more tags
  - Tag autocomplete suggests existing tags
  - Tag cloud shows most frequently used tags
  - Tag changes emit events to Kafka

technical_requirements:
  - New table: tags (id, name, user_id, created_at)
  - New table: task_tags (task_id, tag_id) - many-to-many relationship
  - Update Task Service to handle tag operations
  - Event publishing: task.tagged, task.untagged events to Kafka
  - Frontend: Tag input component with autocomplete
  - MCP tool: add_tags(task_id, tags[]), remove_tags(task_id, tags[])

test_scenarios:
  - Add tags ["work", "urgent"] to task, verify stored
  - Filter tasks by tag "work", verify correct results
  - Remove tag "urgent", verify removed and event published
  - Autocomplete shows existing tags when typing
  - Tag cloud displays most used tags

---

### REQ-005: Search and Filter

priority: high
category: "Core Feature"
epic: "Advanced Task Management"

description: |
  Users can search tasks by title/description and apply multiple filters
  (status, priority, tags, due date) to quickly find relevant tasks.

user_story: |
  As a user, I want to search and filter my tasks so I can quickly find specific items
  among hundreds of tasks.

acceptance_criteria:
  - Full-text search across task title and description
  - Search results highlight matching text
  - Combine filters: status + priority + tags + due date range
  - Save filter presets (e.g., "Work tasks due this week")
  - Search is fast (<100ms) even with 10,000 tasks
  - Sort results by relevance, date, or priority

technical_requirements:
  - PostgreSQL full-text search with GIN index
  - New column: todo.search_vector (tsvector) - auto-updated trigger
  - Task Service: GET /api/todos/search endpoint
  - Query parameters: q (search text), status, priority, tags[], due_before, due_after
  - Frontend: Search bar with filter panel
  - MCP tool: search_tasks(query, filters)

test_scenarios:
  - Search "buy groceries", verify matching tasks
  - Filter: status=pending + priority=high + tag=work
  - Search with 10,000 tasks, verify response < 100ms
  - Save filter preset "Urgent work tasks"
  - Sort search results by due date

---

### REQ-006: Event-Driven Architecture with Kafka

priority: high
category: "Architecture"
epic: "Infrastructure"

description: |
  All task state changes publish events to Kafka, enabling real-time updates,
  audit trails, and future event-driven features (analytics, notifications, webhooks).

user_story: |
  As a developer, I want all task operations to publish events so we can build
  real-time features and maintain a complete audit trail.

acceptance_criteria:
  - All task CRUD operations publish events to Kafka
  - Events include: task.created, task.updated, task.completed, task.deleted
  - Events contain full task payload + metadata (user_id, timestamp)
  - Events are published after database transaction commits
  - Failed event publishing triggers retry with exponential backoff
  - Dead letter queue for permanently failed events
  - Event consumers can process events idempotently

technical_requirements:
  - Kafka cluster with 3 brokers (Strimzi on Kubernetes)
  - Topics: task-events (3 partitions), reminders (1 partition), task-updates (1 partition)
  - Dapr Pub/Sub component configured for Kafka
  - Task Service: EventPublisher class with Dapr HTTP API
  - Outbox pattern for transactional event publishing
  - Event schema validation with JSON Schema

event_schemas:
  task.created:
    task_id: integer
    user_id: string
    title: string
    description: string (optional)
    priority: enum (low, medium, high, critical)
    tags: array of strings
    due_date: ISO 8601 timestamp (optional)
    created_at: ISO 8601 timestamp

  task.updated:
    task_id: integer
    user_id: string
    changes:
      field_name:
        old_value: any
        new_value: any
    updated_at: ISO 8601 timestamp

  task.completed:
    task_id: integer
    user_id: string
    completed_at: ISO 8601 timestamp

  task.deleted:
    task_id: integer
    user_id: string
    deleted_at: ISO 8601 timestamp

test_scenarios:
  - Create task, verify task.created event published to Kafka
  - Update task, verify task.updated event with diff
  - Kafka broker down, verify events buffered and retried
  - Duplicate event delivery, verify idempotent processing
  - Monitor Kafka lag, verify < 1000 messages

---

### REQ-007: Dapr Integration

priority: high
category: "Architecture"
epic: "Infrastructure"

description: |
  Integrate Dapr for distributed application runtime, providing Pub/Sub, State Management,
  Service Invocation, Bindings, and Secrets Management.

user_story: |
  As a developer, I want to use Dapr building blocks so I can abstract infrastructure
  dependencies and simplify distributed systems development.

acceptance_criteria:
  - All services deployed with Dapr sidecars
  - Pub/Sub: Kafka abstraction for event publishing
  - State Store: Redis for caching and distributed state
  - Service Invocation: Dapr-to-Dapr communication
  - Bindings: Cron triggers for Recurrence and Reminder services
  - Secrets: Kubernetes secrets accessed via Dapr API

technical_requirements:
  - Dapr 1.12+ installed on Kubernetes cluster
  - Dapr components defined in YAML:
    - pubsub.yaml (Kafka)
    - statestore.yaml (Redis)
    - secrets.yaml (Kubernetes secrets)
    - bindings.yaml (Cron for scheduled jobs)
  - All services use Dapr SDK or HTTP API
  - Service-to-service calls via Dapr app-id
  - No direct Kafka SDK, Redis SDK, or secret access in code

dapr_components:
  pubsub:
    name: "kafka-pubsub"
    type: "pubsub.kafka"
    metadata:
      - name: "brokers"
        value: "kafka-cluster-kafka-bootstrap:9092"
      - name: "consumerGroup"
        value: "todoapp"

  statestore:
    name: "redis-state"
    type: "state.redis"
    metadata:
      - name: "redisHost"
        value: "redis-master:6379"

  binding:
    name: "cron-recurrence"
    type: "bindings.cron"
    metadata:
      - name: "schedule"
        value: "*/1 * * * *"  # Every minute

test_scenarios:
  - Publish event via Dapr, verify consumed by subscriber
  - Save state via Dapr, retrieve from Redis
  - Call Task Service from Chat Service via Dapr app-id
  - Cron binding triggers Recurrence Service every minute
  - Access DATABASE_URL secret via Dapr secrets API

---

## Part B: Local Deployment

### REQ-008: Minikube Deployment

priority: high
category: "Infrastructure"
epic: "Local Deployment"

description: |
  Deploy the entire TodoApp stack to Minikube for local development and testing,
  including Dapr, Kafka, and all microservices.

acceptance_criteria:
  - Minikube cluster with 4 CPUs, 8GB RAM
  - Dapr installed via Helm chart
  - Kafka deployed via Strimzi Operator
  - All 5 services deployed as Kubernetes Deployments
  - Services accessible via kubectl port-forward
  - Health checks passing for all services
  - End-to-end test passes on Minikube

technical_requirements:
  - Minikube driver: docker or hyperkit
  - Kubernetes 1.28+
  - Helm 3.12+
  - Strimzi Operator 0.38+
  - Dapr 1.12+
  - Helm charts for each service (in k8s/charts/)
  - Skaffold for local development workflow

deployment_steps:
  - Start Minikube: minikube start --cpus=4 --memory=8192
  - Install Dapr: helm install dapr dapr/dapr --namespace dapr-system
  - Install Strimzi: kubectl apply -f strimzi-operator.yaml
  - Deploy Kafka: kubectl apply -f kafka-cluster.yaml
  - Deploy Redis: helm install redis bitnami/redis
  - Deploy services: kubectl apply -f k8s/deployments/
  - Verify: kubectl get pods -A

test_scenarios:
  - Deploy to Minikube, verify all pods running
  - Create task via frontend, verify in database
  - Kafka event flow: create task → event → consumer
  - Dapr service invocation: Chat → Task Service
  - Cron binding triggers Recurrence Service

---

### REQ-009: Local Development Workflow

priority: medium
category: "Developer Experience"
epic: "Local Deployment"

description: |
  Streamlined local development with hot reload, debugging, and easy service restarts.

acceptance_criteria:
  - Docker Compose for local development (without Kubernetes)
  - Skaffold for Kubernetes development (with auto-rebuild)
  - VS Code launch configurations for debugging
  - One-command setup: make dev-start
  - Service logs aggregated with kubectl logs or stern
  - Database migrations run automatically on startup

technical_requirements:
  - docker-compose.yaml with all services
  - skaffold.yaml for Kubernetes dev workflow
  - Makefile with common commands (dev-start, dev-stop, test, deploy)
  - VS Code .vscode/launch.json for Python and Node.js debugging
  - Tilt for advanced Kubernetes dev workflow (optional)

---

## Part C: Cloud Deployment

### REQ-010: Cloud Kubernetes Deployment (AKS/GKE/OKE)

priority: high
category: "Infrastructure"
epic: "Cloud Deployment"

description: |
  Deploy TodoApp to production cloud Kubernetes cluster with high availability,
  auto-scaling, and managed infrastructure.

acceptance_criteria:
  - Kubernetes cluster on AKS, GKE, or OKE
  - Managed Kafka (Confluent Cloud or Redpanda Cloud)
  - Managed Redis (Azure Cache, Google Memorystore, or OCI Cache)
  - Managed PostgreSQL (Neon, Supabase, or cloud provider)
  - Ingress controller with TLS (cert-manager + Let's Encrypt)
  - Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) for all services
  - Prometheus + Grafana for monitoring
  - GitHub Actions CI/CD pipeline

technical_requirements:
  - Terraform or Pulumi for infrastructure as code
  - Helm charts for application deployment
  - GitOps with ArgoCD or FluxCD (optional)
  - TLS certificates via cert-manager
  - External secrets operator for cloud secrets
  - Cloud provider: AKS (Azure), GKE (Google Cloud), or OKE (Oracle Cloud)

deployment_architecture:
  - Ingress (HTTPS) → Next.js Frontend
  - Frontend → Task Service, Chat Service (via Dapr)
  - Task Service → PostgreSQL, Kafka
  - Recurrence Service → Kafka, PostgreSQL (via Dapr Cron)
  - Reminder Service → Kafka (via Dapr Cron)
  - All services → Dapr sidecars → Managed Kafka

test_scenarios:
  - Deploy to cloud cluster, verify HTTPS access
  - Horizontal scaling: load test triggers HPA
  - Pod failure: kill pod, verify auto-recovery
  - Rolling update: deploy new version, verify zero downtime

---

### REQ-011: CI/CD Pipeline

priority: high
category: "Infrastructure"
epic: "Cloud Deployment"

description: |
  Automated CI/CD pipeline with GitHub Actions for testing, building, and deploying
  to staging and production environments.

acceptance_criteria:
  - Pipeline triggered on push to main branch
  - Stages: Build → Test → Deploy Staging → Manual Approval → Deploy Production
  - Docker images pushed to container registry (GitHub Container Registry)
  - Automated tests run on every PR
  - Failed builds block deployment
  - Rollback mechanism for failed deployments

technical_requirements:
  - GitHub Actions workflows in .github/workflows/
  - Workflows: ci.yaml (tests), cd-staging.yaml, cd-production.yaml
  - Secrets stored in GitHub Actions secrets
  - Container registry: ghcr.io (GitHub Container Registry)
  - Helm chart versioning and releases
  - Deployment approval gates for production

pipeline_stages:
  build:
    - Checkout code
    - Run unit tests (pytest)
    - Check code coverage (>80%)
    - Build Docker images
    - Push to ghcr.io

  deploy_staging:
    - Deploy to staging cluster (Helm upgrade)
    - Run smoke tests
    - Run E2E tests (Playwright)

  deploy_production:
    - Manual approval required
    - Deploy to production cluster
    - Run health checks
    - Monitor for 15 minutes
    - Auto-rollback if errors > 5%

test_scenarios:
  - Push to main, verify pipeline runs
  - Failed test blocks deployment
  - Deploy to staging, run E2E tests
  - Approve production deployment
  - Failed deployment triggers rollback

---

### REQ-012: Monitoring and Logging

priority: medium
category: "Observability"
epic: "Cloud Deployment"

description: |
  Comprehensive monitoring, logging, and alerting for production environment.

acceptance_criteria:
  - Prometheus for metrics collection
  - Grafana dashboards for visualization
  - Structured JSON logging from all services
  - Log aggregation with Loki or cloud provider logging
  - Alerts for critical failures (PagerDuty, Slack, email)
  - Distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry + Jaeger

technical_requirements:
  - Prometheus Operator for Kubernetes
  - Grafana dashboards for each service
  - Loki for log aggregation
  - OpenTelemetry collector for traces
  - Alert rules in Prometheus
  - Integration with PagerDuty or Slack

metrics:
  - request_count (counter)
  - request_duration_seconds (histogram)
  - kafka_messages_published (counter)
  - kafka_lag (gauge)
  - database_query_duration (histogram)
  - active_users (gauge)

alerts:
  - Service down > 5 minutes
  - Error rate > 5%
  - Kafka lag > 1000 messages
  - Database query time P95 > 100ms
  - Pod crashlooping

